# Component Architecture Rule (React / Next.js)

- Always follow this architecture when creating React components.
- Use `src/components/effects/cinema-scroll/` component as reference.

--

# 1. Folder Structure

Every reusable UI component must live in its own folder.

- components/
-- ui/
--- component-name/
---- `component-name-subpart.tsx`
---- `component-name.types.ts`
---- `component-name.context.ts`
---- `component-name.styles.ts`
---- `use-component-name.ts`
---- `index.tsx`

Example:

- components/
-- ui/
--- tabs/
---- `tabs-list.tsx`
---- `tabs-trigger.tsx`
---- `tabs-panel.tsx`
---- `tabs.types.ts`
---- `tabs.context.ts`
---- `tabs.styles.ts`
---- `use-tabs.ts`
---- `index.tsx`

> The root component and the public barrel always live together in `index.tsx`. There is no separate root file.

# 2. Naming Conventions

## Files

Use **kebab-case** for all files.

File names follow a **dot vs hyphen** convention:

| Separator | Use for | Examples |
|-----------|---------|---------|
| **Dot** (`.`) | Non-component files — config, context, types, styles, const | `tabs.context.ts`, `tabs.types.ts`, `tabs.const.ts`, `tabs.styles.ts` |
| **Hyphen** (`-`) | Component files — sub-components | `tabs-list.tsx`, `tabs-trigger.tsx`, `tabs-panel.tsx` |

Use a maximum of two words as main component name.

Examples:

Do: `cinema-scroll`
Don't: `marquee-horizontal-scroll`

## Components

Use **PascalCase**.

Examples:

Tabs
TabsList
TabsTrigger
TabsPanel

---

## Hooks

Internal hooks must follow this pattern: `use-component-name.ts`

Examples:

`use-tabs.ts`
`use-modal.ts`
`use-dropdown.ts`

Internal hooks must live **inside the component folder**.

Example:

`components/ui/tabs/use-tabs.ts`

# 3. Barrel Export Rule

Every component folder must contain a **single public entry point**: `index.tsx`.

The root component is defined inside `index.tsx` and composed into the public `Object.assign` export alongside all sub-components.

```tsx
// index.tsx
function TabsRoot({ children, defaultValue }: TabsProps) {
  // Root implementation
}

/** @see {@link TabsRoot} for full usage docs. */
export const Tabs = Object.assign(TabsRoot, {
  List:    TabsList,
  Trigger: TabsTrigger,
  Panel:   TabsPanel,
})

// Also export sub-components directly for consumers who prefer named imports
export { TabsList, TabsTrigger, TabsPanel }
export type * from './tabs.types'
```

Rules:
- Use explicit exports only
- Never use `export *`
- Never create global barrels like `components/index.ts`

# 4. Import Rules

Consumers must import only from the component folder.

Good:
```ts
import { Tabs, TabsList } from "@/components/ui/tabs"
```

Bad:
```ts
import { Tabs } from "@/components/ui/tabs/tabs"
import { TabsList } from "@/components/ui/tabs/tabs-list"
```

Internal files must **never be imported outside the component folder**.

# 5. File Responsibilities

`index.tsx`
Root component, `Object.assign` compound export, and public barrel. All in one file.

`component-name-subpart.tsx`
Compound sub-components. Exported from `index.tsx`.

`component-name.context.ts`
React context and the internal consuming hook. Never exported publicly.

`component-name.types.ts`
TypeScript interfaces and types.

`component-name.styles.ts`
Classnames, style helpers, variants.

`use-component-name.ts`
Internal hooks.

# 6. Example Implementation

- components/
-- ui/
--- tabs/
---- `tabs-list.tsx`
---- `tabs-trigger.tsx`
---- `tabs-panel.tsx`
---- `tabs.types.ts`
---- `tabs.context.ts`
---- `tabs.styles.ts`
---- `use-tabs.ts`
---- `index.tsx`

`index.tsx`:

```tsx
function TabsRoot({ children, defaultValue }: TabsProps) {
  const [active, setActive] = useState(defaultValue)
  return (
    <TabsContext.Provider value={{ active, setActive }}>
      {children}
    </TabsContext.Provider>
  )
}

/** @see {@link TabsRoot} for full usage docs. */
export const Tabs = Object.assign(TabsRoot, {
  List:    TabsList,
  Trigger: TabsTrigger,
  Panel:   TabsPanel,
})

export { TabsList, TabsTrigger, TabsPanel }
export type * from './tabs.types'
```

Usage:

```tsx
import { Tabs } from "@/components/ui/tabs"

<Tabs defaultValue="account">
  <Tabs.List>
    <Tabs.Trigger value="account">Account</Tabs.Trigger>
    <Tabs.Trigger value="password">Password</Tabs.Trigger>
  </Tabs.List>
  <Tabs.Panel value="account">Account content</Tabs.Panel>
  <Tabs.Panel value="password">Password content</Tabs.Panel>
</Tabs>
```

# 7. Private File Enforcement Rule

Files inside a component folder are private by default.

Only what is exported from `index.tsx` is part of the public API.

Allowed:
```ts
import { Tabs } from "@/components/ui/tabs"
```

Not allowed:
```ts
import { TabsContext } from "@/components/ui/tabs/tabs.context"
import { useTabs } from "@/components/ui/tabs/use-tabs"
```

Internal files must only be used **within the same component folder**.

# 8. Subcomponent Composition Rule

All components with multiple parts use the **`Object.assign` compound pattern** — not separate named exports only, not `displayName` hacks, not namespace objects.

```tsx
// index.tsx
/** @see {@link TabsRoot} for full usage docs. */
export const Tabs = Object.assign(TabsRoot, {
  List:    TabsList,
  Trigger: TabsTrigger,
  Panel:   TabsPanel,
})
```

Sub-components are also exported directly as named exports so consumers can import them individually if preferred.

This keeps components:
- scalable
- readable
- easier to maintain

## Context for compound components

When sub-components need to read state from the root (refs, tokens, callbacks), use a `.context.ts` file. The root creates the value and wraps children in the provider. Sub-components consume it via a typed hook that throws outside the provider:

```ts
// tabs.context.ts
export const TabsContext = createContext<TabsContextValue | null>(null)

export function useTabsContext() {
  const ctx = useContext(TabsContext)
  if (!ctx) throw new Error('Tabs sub-components must be rendered inside <Tabs>')
  return ctx
}
```

```tsx
// index.tsx — root wraps children in provider
function TabsRoot({ children, defaultValue }: TabsProps) {
  const [active, setActive] = useState(defaultValue)
  return (
    <TabsContext.Provider value={{ active, setActive }}>
      {children}
    </TabsContext.Provider>
  )
}
```

Sub-components call `useTabsContext()` internally — consumers never import or interact with the context directly.

# 9. Key Principles

1. One component = one folder
2. Root + barrel always in `index.tsx`
3. All compound exports use `Object.assign`
4. Explicit exports only — no `export *`
5. Internal files stay private
6. Consistent naming everywhere
